        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Recoating works when the existing coat is genuinely bonded.</strong> Run the sounding test and a cross-hatch tape test in several places before buying anything, and check the slab for moisture — the original coating's condition, not the new product, decides the outcome.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Abrasion is the job. Cured epoxy is inert and smooth, so mechanical key is the only bond available: buff, grind or sand until no gloss remains anywhere, hand-work the edges, and vacuum twice. Acid etching does nothing at all to cured epoxy.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then test a square metre and give it a week before committing the garage — and once the real coat is down, keep vehicles off for five to seven days, not the 24 hours that make it walkable.
          </p>
